{{smaller block|The priests went up into the steeple, and rang the bells backward, which they call ''tocksaine'', whereupon the people . . . flocked together.
{{right|''Fulke'', Answer to P. Frarine (an. 1580), p. 52. (''Todd.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|That all-softening, overpowering knell,
The ''tocsin'' of the soul—the dinner-bell.
{{right|''Byron'', Don Juan, v. 49.}}}}

{{smaller block|The death of the nominal leader . . . was the ''tocsin'' of their anarchy.
{{right|''Disraeli.''}}}}

2. A bell used to sound an alarm; an alarm-bell.

{{smaller block|Again the whiskered Spaniard all the land with terror smote;
And again the wild alarum sounded from the ''tocsin's'' throat.
{{right|''Longfellow'', Belfry of Bruges.}}}}

3. ''Milit.'', an alarm-drum formerly used as a signal for charging.

'''tocusso''' (tō-ku̇s′ō), ''n.'' [Abyssinian.] An Abyssinian corn-plant or millet, ''Eleusine Tocusso.''

'''tod<sup>1</sup>''' (tod), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''todd, todde, tode''; < ME. ''todd'', < Icel. ''toddi'', a tod of wool, bit, piece, = D. ''todde'', tatters, rags (cf. D. ''toot'', hair-net, Dan. ''tot'', a bunch of hair or flax), = OHG. ''zotta, zotā, zatā'', f., ''zotto'', m., MHG. ''zote, zotte'', m., f., G. ''zotte'', a tuft of hair or wool. Cf. ''tot''<sup>4</sup>.] 1. A bush, especially of ivy; a thick mass of growing foliage.

{{smaller block|At length, within an Yvie ''todde''
(There shrouded was the little God),
I heard a busie bustling.
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., March.}}}}

2†. An old weight, used chiefly for wool and varying in amount locally. It was commonly equal to 28 pounds.

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|And the seid wolle to be wayed in the yelde halle of the seid cite by the byer and the syller, and custom for euery ''todd'' j. d.
{{right|''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 384.}}}}

'''tod<sup>1</sup>†''' (tod), ''v. i.'' [< ''tod''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] To yield a tod in weight; weigh or produce a tod.

{{smaller block|Every 'leven wether ''tods''; every tod yields pound and odd shilling; fifteen hundred shorn, what comes the wool to?
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 3. 33.}}}}

'''tod<sup>2</sup>''' (tod), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''todde''; supposed to be so called from its bushy tail, < ''tod''<sup>1</sup>, a bush.] A fox. [Old Eng. and Scotch.]

{{smaller block|Drivest hence the wolf, the ''tod'', the brock,<br>Or other vermin from the flock.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Pan's Anniversary.}}
Frae dogs, an' ''tods'', an' butchers' knives!
{{right|''Burns'', Death of Mailie.}}}}

'''tod<sup>3</sup>''' (tod), ''n.'' [Abbr. of ''toddy''.] A drink; toddy. [Colloq., U. S.]

{{smaller block|Selleridge's was full of fire-company boys, taking their ''tods'' after a run.
{{right|''T. Winthrop'', Cecil Dreeme, xiv.}}}}

'''todash†''', ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''todasshen'', ''todaisshen''; < ''to-''<sup>2</sup> + ''dash''.] To strike violently; dash to pieces.

{{smaller block|His shelde ''to-dasshed'' was with swerdes and maces.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 640.}}
Well it semed by their armes that thei hadde not soiourned, ffor theire sheildes were hewen and ''to daissht''.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 246.}}}}

'''to-day, today''' (tọ̈-dā′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''to-daye'', ''to daye'', < AS. ''tō dæge'', ''tō dæg'' (also ''tō dæge thissum''), on (this) day: prop. a phrase: ''tō'', prep., to, for, on; ''dæge'', dat. sing. of ''dæg'', day: see ''to''<sup>1</sup> and ''day''. Cf. ''to-night'', ''to-morrow'', ''to-month'', ''to-year''.] '''1.''' On this (present) day: as, he leaves ''to-day''. Compare ''to-morrow''.

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|To-morrow let my Sun his beams display,<br>Or in clouds hide them; I have lived ''to-day.''
{{right|''Cowley'', A Vote.}}}}

2. At the present time; in these days.

{{smaller block|Man ''to-day'' is fancy's fool<br>As man hath ever been.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Ancient Sage.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To-day morning''', this morning. [Prov. Eng.]—'''To-day noon''', this noon. [U. S.]}}

¶
'''to-day, today''' (tọ̈-dā′), ''n.'' [< ''to-day'', ''adv.''] 1. This present day: as, ''to-day'' is Monday.—2. This present time; the present age: as, the events of ''to-day''.

