'''toddy''' (tod′i), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''taddy'', also ''taree''; < Hind. ''tāri'' (with cerebral ''r'', hence also spelled ''tādi''), < ''tār'', Pers. ''tār'', a palm-tree, from which this liquor is derived.] 1. The drawn sap of several species of palm, especially when fermented. {{smaller|In India this is obtained chiefly from the jaggery, the wild date, the palmyra, and the cocoanut (see ''toddy-palm''); in Borneo, from the areng; in West Africa, from ''Raphia vinifera''; in Brazil, from the buriti. It is secured by cutting off the spadix at the time of efflorescence, by wounding the spathe, and by tapping the pith. It is a pleasant laxative drink when fresh, but soon ferments, and becomes intoxicating. Arrack is obtained from it by distillation. Vinegar is also made from the sap, and jaggery-sugar is obtained by boiling it.}}

{{smaller block|They [the people of Industan] have . . . also ''Taddy'', an excellent Drink that issues out of a tree.
{{right|''S. Clarke'', Geograph. Descrip. (1671), p. 45.}}}}

{{smaller block|If we had a mind to Coco-nuts, or ''Toddy'', our Malayans of Achin would climb the Trees, and fetch as many Nuts as we would have, and a good pot of ''Toddy'' every Morning.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, I. 488.}}}}

2. A drink made of spirits and hot water sweetened, and properly having no other ingredients: this use is originally Scotch. Also colloquially ''tod''.

